TI  - COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS OF MACHINE LEARNING ALGORITHMS AND PHYSICAL MODELS FOR SOIL MOISTURE RETRIEVAL USING UNMANNED AERIAL VEHICLES
T2  - 2025 IEEE INTERNATIONAL GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING SYMPOSIUM (IGARSS)
LA  - English
KW  - soil moisture retrieval; unmanned aerial vehicle; L-band microwave radiometer; machine learning models; precision agriculture
AB  - Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) equipped with microwave radiometers offer significant advantages in soil moisture retrieval due to their high spatial resolution, cost-effectiveness, and flexibility. This study focuses on the use of an L-band microwave radiometer, PoLRa, mounted on a UAV for long-term monitoring of soil moisture in the Saihanba region of Hebei, China. The research combines brightness temperature data with vegetation, soil, and temperature information to develop four retrieval models: Random Forest (RF), Back Propagation Neural Network (BPNN), Generalized Linear Regression (GLR), alongside a physical model, the L-band Microwave Emission of the Biosphere (L-MEB) model, for soil moisture retrieval. Validation against ground observations showed that the RF model provided the most accurate results, particularly in areas with low clay content and vegetation. The accuracy of soil moisture retrieval varied across landcover types, with higher precision in bareland and farmland. These results demonstrate the potential of UAV-based methods for soil moisture monitoring in agriculture and environmental applications.
