AU  - Liu, H; Chen, JY; Bian, J; Li, ZL; Zhang, WJ; Yang, N; Du, RQ; Qian, L; Geng, HS; Chen, YW; Zhang, ZT; Shi, LS
TI  - Enhancing field-scale soil moisture content monitoring using UAV hyperspectral-derived multi-dimensional spectral response indices of crop comprehensive phenotypic traits
T2  - COMPUTERS AND ELECTRONICS IN AGRICULTURE
LA  - English
KW  - UAV hyperspectral imaging; Crop phenotyping; Soil moisture content (SMC); Fuzzy comprehensive evaluation (FCE); Multi-dimensional spectral response indices; WATER
AB  - Accurate and timely estimation of soil moisture content (SMC) at the field scale is crucial for optimizing irrigation decisions. Although remote sensing has emerged as an effective method for monitoring SMC in farmland through crop phenotype observation, existing studies have not fully exploited the potential of phenotypic trait parameters (PTPs) in this context. To enhance the accuracy and reliability of SMC monitoring models, it is essential to systematically integrate PTPs and analyze their spectral response features. This study aims to improve maize SMC monitoring models by utilizing multi-dimensional spectral response features derived from crop PTPs. First, a comprehensive phenotypic trait index (CPTI) was developed using the fuzzy comprehensive evaluation (FCE) method. This index incorporates key PTPs, including the leaf area index (LAI), aboveground biomass (AGB), leaf chlorophyll content (LCC), and leaf water content (LWC). The weights of individual PTPs within the CPTI were adjusted based on their sensitivity to water stress, to establish a water-stress-specific comprehensive phenotypic trait index (CPTIws). Afterwards, fractional-order differentiation (FOD) and optimal band combination (OBC) were used to extract multi-dimensional spectral response features of each PTP. These features were then evaluated for their effectiveness in monitoring SMC using various machine learning models. The results show that: (1) CPTIws effectively integrates the spectral responses of PTPs, achieving an absolute Pearson correlation coefficient (|PCCs|) of 0.77 with SMC, higher than that of individual PTPs (|PCCs| = 0.42-0.60) and CPTI (|PCCs| = 0.72). (2) The combination of FOD and OBC enhances the spectral response of each PTP, increasing the |PCC| by an average of 0.30; (3) The highest SMC monitoring accuracy was achieved by inputting the multi-dimensional spectral response features of CPTIws in a stacked ensemble learning model (R2 = 0.84,RMSE = 0.014), outperforming the best individual PTP, AGB (R2 = 0.76, RMSE = 0.021 cm center dot cm-3). In summary, multi-dimensional spectral response features derived from CPTIws, using UAV-based hyperspectral imagery, significantly improve the accuracy of field-scale SMC monitoring for maize. This study provides a solid scientific basis for accurately obtaining farmland SMC.
