TI  - Precision Agriculture in the Era of Technological Advancement
T2  - Artificial Intelligence and Data Sciences for Precision Agriculture
LA  - English
KW  - Drone technology; GIS; GPS; IoT and crop management; Precision agriculture; Remote sensing; Crops; Decision making; Food supply; Harvesters; Internet of things; Learning systems; Precision agriculture; Predictive analytics; Soil surveys; Technological forecasting; Tractors (agricultural); Crop managements; Crop yield; Cutting edge technology; Drone technology; Geospatial tools; IoT and crop management; Precision Agriculture; Remote-sensing; Resource efficiencies; Technological advancement; Remote sensing
AB  - Precision agriculture (PA) has transformed contemporary farming by incorporating cutting-edge technologies to optimize crop yields, improve resource efficiency, and foster sustainability. With the rapid advancement of geospatial tools, artificial intelligence, the Internet of Things, and automation, precision agriculture enables site-specific management based on real-time data analysis. GIS and GPS facilitate precise field mapping, soil variability assessment, and targeted input applications, reducing costs and environmental impacts. Remote sensing, drones, and satellite imagery further improve crop monitoring, allowing early detection of stress factors such as nutrient deficiencies, pests, and diseases. The use of smart sensors and IoT devices allows for real-time monitoring of soil moisture, temperature, and weather conditions, facilitating more efficient irrigation and fertilization strategies. AI-driven predictive analytics assist in decision-making by analyzing vast agricultural datasets, improving yield forecasting, and mitigating risks associated with climate variability. Autonomous machinery, including GPS-guided tractors and robotic harvesters, boosts efficiency and reduces labour needs. However, the broad adoption of precision agriculture is hindered by challenges such as high upfront costs, complex data management, and a demand for specialized technical skills. Future developments in machine learning, blockchain technology, and 5G connectivity are expected to further enhance precision farming by ensuring data security, real-time communication, and seamless automation. The role of technological advancements in precision agriculture is crucial for enhancing productivity, promoting sustainability, and ensuring the future of global food security. By adopting innovative tools, farmers can optimize agricultural practices, reduce resource wastage, and ensure resilient food production systems. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
