TI  - Smart Agriculture Monitoring System Using Edge Multimodal Fusion Framework
T2  - 2nd International Conference on Intelligent Algorithms for Computational Intelligence Systems, IACIS 2025
LA  - English
KW  - artificial intelligence; edge multimodal fusion framework; internet of things; smart agriculture monitoring system; unmanned aerial vehicle; Aerial photography; Agricultural machinery; Antennas; Crops; Distributed computer systems; Feature extraction; Interpolation; Irrigation; Learning systems; Neural networks; Smart agriculture; Aerial vehicle; Agriculture monitoring; Edge multimodal fusion framework; Health management; Monitoring system; Multi-modal fusion; Resource management; Smart agriculture monitoring system; Smart agricultures; Unmanned aerial vehicle; Internet of things; Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV)
AB  - Currently, smart agriculture increasingly depends on drones, Internet of Things (IoT) devices, and edge Artificial Intelligence (AI) to deliver timely insights into crop health and resource management. However, existing Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV)-assisted Tiny Machine Learning (ML) frameworks for soil moisture forecasting are limited to single-sensor modalities, which leads to a reduced ability to capture complex crop conditions and incomplete situational awareness in dynamic agricultural environments. Hence, this paper proposes an Edge Multimodal Fusion Framework (EMFF) that integrates UAV-captured aerial imagery with time-series data from ground IoT sensors. Initially, multimodal data were collected using synchronized UAV flyovers and in-field sensing. Then, bilinear interpolation, min-max scaling, and linear interpolation are employed to resize images, normalize pixel values, and sample the sensor readings to ensure data consistency. MobileNetV3 was used for visual feature extraction and gated feature concatenation was performed to integrate the sensor and image embeddings for localized model adaptation. Next, 8-bit quantization compresses the model for efficient over-the-air deployment. Finally, rulebased thresholding on-node allows autonomous irrigation, pest alerts, and stress monitoring. From the results, the proposed EMFF established superior results in terms of accuracy (98.8%) compared to the existing lightweight Convolutional Neural Network (TinyLiteNet) model. © 2025 IEEE.
