TI  - SAMS: a sustainable agri-tech monitoring system integrating IoT and TinyML for smart farming
T2  - Journal of Reliable Intelligent Environments
LA  - English
KW  - Ardino; Computer vision; Deep learning; Drone; Internet of Things; Leaf disease; Soil moisture; Agri-food; Agricultural machinery; Climate change; Crops; Cultivation; Decision making; Food supply; Learning systems; Population statistics; Statistical tests; Sustainable agriculture; Sustainable development; %moisture; Ardino; Crop managements; Deep learning; Global food security; Interpretability; Leaf disease; Machine-learning; Monitoring system; Security Economics; Deep learning
AB  - Agriculture is a foundational industry that supports global food security, economic stability and environmental health. It encompasses the cultivation of crops and the raising of livestock, playing a critical role in providing essential resources and services. As the world faces increasing challenges such as climate change, resource depletion and population growth, there is a pressing need for innovative solutions to enhance agricultural productivity and sustainability. Our advanced agricultural monitoring system, the Sustainable Agri-tech monitoring system (SAMS), combines Internet of Things (IoT) devices and Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) technology to tackle critical issues in the farming industry. SAMS automates water management, optimizes irrigation and monitors environmental conditions, all powered by a sustainable energy system. The system is designed for future integration with drone technology to enhance remote monitoring, early disease detection and overall resource efficiency. We also developed a novel TinyML-based model, DeepNet, which achieved 97.53% accuracy in classifying potato leaf diseases, showcasing the potential of combining IoT and AI for improved decision-making and crop management. Grad-CAM was employed to enhance model interpretability, confirming the practicality and effectiveness of this approach in promoting a resilient and productive agricultural future. In this paper, we proposed a novel model (DeepNet) to classify potato leaf diseases using TinyML. The model, trained on a broad dataset including different leaf conditions, had outstanding accuracy, with a testing accuracy of 97.53%. This model plays a crucial role in detecting agricultural diseases by demonstrating how combining IoT and AI models, such as SAMS, improves decision-making, crop management and disease detection, eventually fostering a resilient and productive agricultural future. We employed gradient-weighted (Grad-CAM) class activation mapping to enhance model interpretability to generate heatmaps, highlighting areas contributing to the model’s predictions. The experimental results confirmed the competitive performance, validity and practicality of the proposed approach. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
