KW  - Climate adaptation; Heritage landscape; Hydrological restoration; Nasrid gardens; Regenerative aesthetics; Sustainable landscape renewal; Conservation of Natural Resources; Ecosystem; Hydrology; Rain; Climate models; Ecology; Historic preservation; Infiltration; Land reclamation; Rain; Soil testing; Water conservation; environmental DNA; ground water; rain; stable isotope; storm water; rain; Alhambra; Climate adaptation; Heritage landscape; Hydrological restoration; Nasrid garden; Raingarden; Regenerative esthetic; Sustainable landscape; Sustainable landscape renewal; Water sensitive; conceptual framework; esthetics; heritage conservation; hydrological regime; landscape; quantitative analysis; runoff; sustainable development; water management; Article; Bayes theorem; climate model; computational fluid dynamics; computer simulation; conceptual framework; conservation biology; drought; ecological diversity; ecosystem restoration; environmental management; environmental monitoring; evapotranspiration; flooding; flow measurement; geographic information system; geographic mapping; history; hydrology; irrigation (agriculture); isotope analysis; land use; landscape; landscape ecology; microclimate; pollinator; process optimization; quantitative analysis; relative humidity; remote sensing; soil degradation; soil infiltration; soil moisture; species richness; Storm Water Management Model; surface runoff; thermography; topography; unmanned aerial vehicle; vegetation; water flow; water management; ecosystem; environmental protection; hydrology; procedures; Restoration
AB  - This research develops a regenerative framework for the renewal of the Alhambra landscape, integrating distributed rain-garden systems with hydrological restoration under the conceptual premise of Dialogue Between Palace and Land. The study formulates an adaptive model that reconciles heritage conservation with climate-responsive water management. Confronted with increasing drought, episodic flooding, and soil degradation, the Alhambra is reinterpreted as both a testing ground and a new conceptual framework that functions as a metaphor for water-sensitive heritage design, where ecological performance and cultural imagination converge. GIS-based terrain and flow analyses delineate priority zones for decentralized rain-garden interventions, while field infiltration testing and UAV-derived NDVI mapping evaluate ecological response. Quantitative results indicate up to a 30 % reduction in surface runoff and a 15 % increase in infiltration capacity, accompanied by significant gains in vegetative vitality. The selective reactivation of historical channels and passive irrigation conduits revives a dormant ecological memory, reconnecting the site's historical hydraulic intelligence with contemporary regenerative practice. Beyond technical outcomes, the study conceptualizes water as a regenerative aesthetic medium that operates with dual agency—as ecological infrastructure and as cultural expression—through which form, ecology, and perception are interwoven. Within this synthesis, the Alhambra transcends its role as a historical relic to emerge as an operative prototype of regenerative landscape, demonstrating resilience through beauty and adaptation through memory, and positioning itself as a paradigm of water-sensitive heritage intervention. © 2026 The Author
