AU  - Shi, H.; Xiang, Y.; Tang, Z.; Du, R.; Sun, T.; Liu, X.; Huang, X.; Li, Z.; Zhang, F.
TI  - Improving surface soil moisture content estimation of winter oilseed rape by integrating multimodal remote sensing information and structural characteristics
T2  - Computers and Electronics in Agriculture
LA  - English
KW  - Information fusion; Multimodal; Soil moisture content; Structural characteristics; Winter oilseed rape; Backpropagation; Classification (of information); Forestry; Image enhancement; Image texture; Information fusion; Infrared devices; Infrared radiation; Neural networks; Oilseeds; Remote sensing; Sensor data fusion; Soil surveys; Textures; Uncertainty analysis; Vegetation; Growth stages; Multi-modal; Oil seed rape; Remote sensing information; Soil moisture content; Spectral information; Structural characteristics; Thermal-infrared; Vegetation index; Winter oilseed rape; canola; complementarity; crop plant; crop production; growth rate; NDVI; remote sensing; soil moisture; winter; Soil moisture
AB  - Multimodal remote sensing information fusion, as a significant branch of modern remote sensing technology, offers the advantage of integrating spectral information from various sensors, thereby providing more comprehensive and precise data interpretation. This not only enhances data complementarity but also improves the accuracy and reliability of target detection and classification. This study employed multispectral and thermal infrared cameras mounted on drones to acquire remote sensing images and to measure soil moisture content (SMC) on the surface of winter oilseed rape canopies at various growth stages from 2022 to 2023. Structural characteristics (SC) were extracted from RGB images, and texture feature indices (TFIs) and thermal infrared indices (TIs) were constructed using texture features (TFs) and thermal infrared features (T), respectively. Additionally, a novel stacked vegetation index (SVIs) was created based on vegetation indices (VIs). By employing multimodal remote sensing information fusion techniques, these indices were categorized into different fusion combinations to develop predictive models for SMC at various growth stages of winter oilseed rape. The study found that the novel stacked vegetation index exhibited a strong correlation with SMC (correlation coefficient R > 0.8 during the budding stage). Compared to models without structural characteristics integration, the performance of multispectral, thermal infrared, and multimodal remote sensing models improved to varying degrees after incorporating structural characteristics. By integrating multimodal remote sensing information (multispectral indices including vegetation and texture indices, thermal infrared indices, and structural characteristics) with a Backpropagation Neural Network (BPNN), the predictive model for SMC at various growth stages of winter oilseed rape achieved optimal performance (seedling stage: R2 = 0.732, RMSE = 0.0117, RPD = 1.905; budding stage: R2 = 0.826, RMSE = 0.009, RPD = 2.777; flowering stage: R2 = 0.793, RMSE = 0.0102, RPD = 2.221). The results of this study enhance the scientific understanding and accurate modeling of the spectral information of winter oilseed rape canopies. They provide a theoretical foundation for the inversion of crop growth and SMC management through multimodal remote sensing information fusion techniques. This advancement supports applications in agriculture, forestry, and geological exploration by offering more precise data support in these fields. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
