TI  - Integrating IOT And AI For End-To-End Agricultural Intelligence Systems
T2  - International Conference on Engineering, Technology and Management, ICETM 2025
LA  - English
KW  - Agricultural Intelligence Systems; Artificial Intelligence; Crop Yield Prediction; Edge Computing; Internet of Things; Precision Farming; Sensor Networks; Smart Agriculture; Agricultural machinery; Convolution; Crops; Deep learning; Edge computing; Food supply; Irrigation; Learning systems; Neural networks; Population statistics; Precision agriculture; Smart agriculture; Agricultural intelligence system; Crop yield; Crop yield prediction; Edge computing; End to end; Intelligence systems; Precision-farming; Sensors network; Smart agricultures; Yield prediction; Internet of things; Sensor networks
AB  - The present-day global agricultural sector has been faced with numerous problems related to world population growth, climate change, diminishing natural resources, and labour shortages. The traditional ways of farming are less likely to meet the increasing demands of food security, sustainable farming and operational efficiency. In such context, inclusion of Internet of Things (IoT) technologies and artificial intelligence approaches creates a transformation. In this study, the author proposes comprehensive end-to-end agricultural intelligence in which IoT sensor-networked environmental sensing and edge computing, along with AI-powered smart farming analytics, collaborates with performance enhancement in farming operations. The aforementioned architecture encloses the sensor network for real-time soil monitoring like soil moisture, temperature, humidity, crop health, and weather condition so that AI algorithms like convolutional neural networks (CNNs), support vector machines (SVMs), as well as deep-learning-based regressors can play a significant role in various applications such as yield prediction, disease diagnosis, and intelligent irrigation scheduling. The predicted system was evaluated with huge real datasets from PlantVillage, IEEE-CIS Crop Yield, and AgroDataCube against accuracy of prediction, communication efficiency, and resource usage. Service results confirmed that AI-improved models have significantly outshone traditional baseline models, whereby AI-improved models boast 18.4 % accuracy improvement in crop yield prediction, F1-scores up to 0.91 in detecting pests/diseases, and around 23% water savings through AI-aided irrigation. Edge computing also greatly enhances latency, with inference time lessening by 45% compared to cloud-only methods, thus enabling real-time decision making in a rural or power connectivity scenario. This study illustrates how an IoT-AI integration in a scalable, modular architecture may possibly trigger precision agriculture and sustainable farming. Practical challenges for implementation in the field include sensor calibration, energy management, protection of data security, and model generalization across crops and regions. Many potential research fronts were raised, such as federated learning for frameworks in decentralized farming practiced by land users, integrating blockchain to ensure food traceability, and coordination of artificial intelligence of things unchaperoned by drone-manned systems. Through the merger of data collection and intelligent decision-making, the study presents an analytical organization for the modernization of agriculture, thanks to potential use of next-generation computing technologies. © 2025 IEEE.
