TI  - Smart Irrigation: Harnessing AI for Sustainable Agriculture
T2  - Smart Agriculture for Sustainable Practices: The Role of AI, IoT, and Machine Learning
LA  - English
KW  - Agricultural machinery; Crops; Data accuracy; Fertilizers; Food supply; Information management; Internet of things; Irrigation; Large datasets; Learning systems; Machine learning; Smart agriculture; Sustainable agriculture; Water management; Weather forecasting; Applications of AI; Crop yield; Data analytics; Irrigation systems; Learning data; Machine-learning; Sustainable agriculture; System use; Water efficiency'; Water usage; Remote sensing
AB  - Artificial intelligence integrated into smart irrigation techniques is transforming the agricultural domain by increasing water efficiency, crop yield, and sustainability. This chapter finds the application of AI-based technologies toward smart irrigation through machine learning, data analytics, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices to optimize water usage in farming. AI-based irrigation systems use real-time data from sensors, weather forecasts, and satellite imagery to make intelligent decisions for watering. Irrigation management system uses large datasets and machine learning models to learn from previous patterns to increase the accuracy of irrigation management. Additionally, drones and remote sensing technologies combined with AI can be used to monitor crop conditions precisely to support efficient water management. This chapter addresses one of the key challenges: how to collect and manage data. High-quality data is required for AI-driven smart irrigation methods to work efficiently. IoT Devices such as temperature sensors, soil moisture sensors, and weather stations provide necessary data, but work requires ensuring data accuracy and handling a huge amount of data to be implemented. This chapter also looks at how AI could contribute to lowering the environmental footprint of agriculture by decreasing water consumption, wastage, and reducing the use of chemical fertilizers, using irrigation strategies aimed at certain areas. The discussion finishes with the direction of future smart irrigation based on AI, such as the development of more advanced and commonly available algorithms, the rapid increase in motivations for smart irrigation, and advancements in AI for solving global food security problems. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, S.N. Kumar, Sherin Zafar, Arun S, Sameena Naaz, M. M. Sufyan Beg and Eduard Babulak; individual chapters, the contributors.
