AB  - They involve monitoring and analyzing daanalyzeeal-time, real-time farmers to derive key insights on their crops or soil at any given time. You install IoT sensors in several locations across the farm that measure temperature, humidity, soil moisture, etc. The telemetry is then transmitted to a central database or cloud platform for processing, storage, and analysis. A farmer can, in near real-time, for instance, decide to change the timing of irrigation based on measured soil moisture and how much water is required. Drones can also provide a high-level image of the farm using specialized cameras, sensors, and on-ground sensors. They are capable of taking high-defbig-bitmaps-big and sensing subtle changes. They can send the information to a cloud server where farmers can red-flag areas they feel might need attention so booms or sprayers addressing liquid dirt drenchers travel. Brparriti. Drones can also be used for crop spraying to minimize manual labor and increase efficiency. IoT and Sensor-Enabled Drones in Agriculture Crop Monitoring: There is a huge potential in using IoT & sensor-caped drones for precision agriculture and crop monitoring. They also have the potential to revolutionize farming techniques, making them more sustainable, efficient, and cheap. Large-scale data collection and analysis capabilities allow farmers to optimize yields through data-driven decision-making. Precision agriculture and crop monitoring Since farmers can make the best decisions based on real-time data, it is one of the most exciting IoT applications in the internet landscape. These technologies allow data about crops and soil levels to be instantaneously and analyzed quickly. IoT sensors can measure the temperature, humidity, soil moisture, and other parameters across farms. The data could then be sent to a central database or cloud for analysis and further decision-making based on the studies. Therefore, agriculture farmers can determine the optimal irrigation schedule based on real-time soil moisture levels. Thus, water is being conserved, and even more important, crop yield will be increased. Apart from the on-ground sensors and systems, aerial drones equipped with specialized cameras/sensors can get an overall bird-eye-view of the farm. This way, they can take high-resolution images and notice slight changes in the vegetation of the crops to identify possible problematic areas and focus on them. © 2025 IEEE.
