AU  - Reddy, N.K.; Purushotham, P.; Jha, S.; Rakhonde, G.Y.; Reddy, N.P.; Reddy, S.S.
TI  - Drones: The unmanned aerial vehicles for precision management of pests and diseases of crops
T2  - Optimizing Smart and Sustainable Agriculture for Sustainability
LA  - English
KW  - Advanced Analytics; Agri-food; Agricultural chemicals; Agricultural implements; Big data; Crops; Cultivation; Marketing; Noninvasive medical procedures; Precision agriculture; Public policy; Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV); Aerial vehicle; Agriculture sectors; Decisions makings; Disease management; Farm mechanization; Global players; Gross value added; Informed decision; Pest management; Resources utilizations; Decision making
AB  - India is a prominent global player in the agriculture sector, with approximately 58 per cent of its population dependent on agriculture. In FY 2021-22, agriculture and allied exports touched US $50.21 billion, contributing 18.8 per cent to the gross value added. Government policies are geared towards optimizing resource utilization in farm mechanization, precision agriculture, agri-marketing, and processing. The advent of Agriculture 4.0 includes big data analytics, Internet of Things (IoT), drones, and sensors, and is anticipated to revolutionize the agri-food sector. Modern farming practices in India are increasingly embracing drones for their precision and efficiency. Drones play a crucial role in various farm operations, including crop health monitoring, agrochemical spraying, yield forecasting, and the promotion of smart agriculture. This technological shift is enhancing productivity and sustainability in Indian agriculture. Drones, also referred to as unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), are popular in agriculture due to their capability for detailed mapping, monitoring, and decision-making capabilities. In situations with extensive reliance on pesticide spraying for crop pest and disease management, drones boost spray efficiency and mitigate operational risks for applicators. Drones can also be used to create highly detailed maps of agricultural fields, providing farmers with a better understanding of the layout, topography, and soil composition of their land. This information will help farmers in decisions about planting, irrigation, and fertilization that lead to establish a healthy crop. Drones can also be used to monitor crops throughout the growing season. With high-resolution cameras and sensors, drones can detect changes in crop health, soil moisture, pest and disease infestations. The herbivory by pests and infestation by fungal, bacterial, and viral diseases induces physiological defence or susceptible reaction in plants leads to change in leaf reflectance. The unconventional imaging technologies can detect such fluctuations, serving as non-invasive methods for monitoring crops. This enables the early identification of potential issues, empowering farmers to take proactive measures before problems escalate. It also provides farmers with data-driven insights, allowing them to make more informed decisions about their operations. For instance, if a drone detects a crop that is under stress, farmer can use that information to adjust management practices. For the widespread adoption of drones in commercial agriculture, growers seek proven, cost-effective technology aligned with established practices. Efficient pest and disease monitoring, currently hindered by time constraints and lacking reliable sampling techniques, poses a critical research challenge. Drones are pivotal in enhancing agricultural efficiency, cost reduction, and informed decision-making. Sustainable pest and disease management in the 21st-century agriculture hinges on innovative skills, driving the need for multidisciplinary research collaborations. © © 2025 Biswaranjan Acharya, Ankita Bansal, Abha Jain, Rachna Jain and Joel J. P. C. Rodrigues. All rights reserved.
