AU  - Mahto, M.K.; Srivastava, S.K.; Sah, B.
TI  - Introduction to Smart Farming: Definition, Importance and Trends
T2  - Smart Agritech: Robotics, AI, and Internet of Things (IoT) in Agriculture
LA  - English
KW  - climate change; crop management; data analysis; drones; Internet of Things (IoT); sensors; Smart farming; sustainability
AB  - Smart farming is a cutting-edge agricultural approach that integrates advanced technologies to enhance efficiency, productivity, and sustainability. This method addresses the rising global demand for food due to population growth while aiming to conserve natural resources and mitigate climate change impacts. Utilizing technologies such as sensors, drones, robots, precision agriculture, and the Internet of Things (IoT), smart farming collects and analyses data to make informed decisions and optimize agricultural processes. By monitoring factors like soil moisture, temperature, nutrients, and pests, smart farming empowers farmers to manage crop growth more effectively. This technology-driven approach reduces input costs, boosts yields, and minimizes environmental impacts, presenting a sustainable alternative to traditional farming practices. With the global population projected to reach 9.7 billion by 2050, a 70% increase in food production will be necessary. Traditional methods, which often lead to soil degradation, water pollution, and biodiversity loss, are insufficient to meet this demand. Smart farming offers a viable solution by enabling higher food production with fewer resources. Precision agriculture is a significant aspect of smart farming, involving the collection of data on soil, crops, and weather through sensors, drones, and satellite imagery. This data is used to create detailed field maps, allowing for tailored farming techniques for specific zones. Another critical component is the use of autonomous robots and drones, which can automate labour-intensive tasks such as planting, harvesting, and monitoring crops. These technologies operate around the clock, providing real-time data and enabling rapid responses to changing conditions. The IoT plays a crucial role in smart farming by using sensors and devices to gather data, which is then analyzed centrally. This application includes monitoring soil moisture and temperature, managing irrigation systems, and optimizing equipment use. IoT integration helps farmers refine their practices and reduce waste, contributing to greater sustainability. © 2024 Scrivener Publishing LLC.
