AU  - Huacles, G.C.O.; Marino, A.; Kirk, W.; Oliveira, L.P.; Neto, J.R.M.; Goés, J.A.; Hernandez-Figueroa, H.E.
TI  - Soil Moisture Monitoring for Precision Agriculture via Drone-Borne Multi-Band Synthetic Aperture Radar
T2  - 2025 Photonics and Electromagnetics Research Symposium - Spring, PIERS-Spring 2025 - Proceedings
LA  - English
KW  - Drones; Irrigation; Moisture determination; Personnel; Precision agriculture; Radar measurement; Remote sensing; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Vegetation; %moisture; C-bands; Gravimetric sampling; Ground sensors; High costs; Labour intensity; Multi band; Precision Agriculture; Reliable measurement; Soil moisture monitoring; Synthetic aperture radar
AB  - Soil moisture monitoring is crucial for optimizing irrigation strategies to meet crop requirements. However, traditional methods, such as gravimetric sampling with ground sensors, are often constrained by high costs, labor intensity, and limited scalability for large areas. Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) has emerged as a transformative tool for soil moisture monitoring, offering reliable measurements across diverse terrains and under challenging conditions, including cloud cover and dense vegetation. Unlike optical and thermal techniques, SAR penetrates vegetation and interacts with soil properties, enabling multi-depth moisture estimation through multiple frequency bands (C-band, L-band, and P-band). This study employs a drone-borne SAR system to estimate soil moisture in agricultural soils using L, P, and C bands. The modified Dubois method is applied to facilitate soil moisture monitoring in both bare and vegetated areas. The results demonstrate the generation of high-resolution soil moisture maps, highlighting the potential of remote sensing technologies for enhancing modern irrigation management.  © 2025 IEEE.
