AU  - Garg, P.K.
TI  - Spatial data acquisition methods for agricultural monitoring
T2  - Agricultural Insights from Space: Machine Learning Applications in Satellite Data Analysis
LA  - English
KW  - agriculture sector; cloud computing; GIS; GPS; IoT; LiDAR; Remote sensing; UAV; Agricultural economics; Agricultural implements; Agricultural machinery; Big data; Cloud analytics; Crops; Data communication systems; Information use; Internet of things; Man machine systems; Mapping; Personnel; Population statistics; Precision agriculture; Soil moisture; Soil surveys; Aerial vehicle; Agriculture sectors; Cloud-computing; Crop productivity; Crop yield; Global positioning; LiDAR; Positioning system; Remote-sensing; Unmanned aerial vehicle; Geographic information systems; Remote sensing; Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV)
AB  - According to Food and Agriculture Organization, food production is required to be enhanced by 60% by year 2050 to meet the requirements of expanding population, which is anticipated to reach 9 billion. Increased agricultural productivity is a global challenge, but smart farming involving information communication technologies, Internet of Things (IoT), unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV), satellite images, Global Positioning System (GPS), AI, machine learning, cloud computing, and big data analytics can be employed to increase not only the crop productivity for growing population but also the income of farmers for socio-economic development and better living. In addition, precision agriculture offers various ways and means to optimize the requirements of fertilization and water, improve the quality of agricultural produce, and increase the crop productivity. The range of IoT applications in agriculture is large. For a long time, remote sensing (RS) technology has been used in the agriculture sector for mapping and monitoring various agricultural activities, such as soil mapping, crop mapping, soil moisture estimation, crop water requirement modeling, crop yield estimation, crop disease monitoring, and site suitability for agriculture. As technology changes, RS high-resolution images along with GPS, LiDAR, IoT, UAV, and sensors are being employed in agriculture sector for various tasks, such as crop yield estimation, crop phenological information, detection of diseased crops, and monitoring crop fields. Previously, the agriculture was fully dependent on human resources and hard machinery, but it has now started applying technological solutions and modernizing its various operations with IoT applications, which are supposed to make a significant impact on agriculture. One of the popular applications of IoT in agriculture is the precision farming with the adoption of smart technology that includes sensors, robots, systems of control, and autonomous vehicles. These spatial data, thus collected, can be analyzed in geographic information system or on a cloud platform to get the results for effective monitoring of agricultural areas. This chapter discusses various modern tools to collect, analyze, and monitor the agricultural information to enhance the crop productivity. It also briefly describes some of the potential applications of these tools in agriculture sector. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved..
