KW  - a relay; AM2302 soil moisture sensor; an Arduino Uno; ESP8266; IoT; Digital storage; Farms; Moisture control; Network security; Remote sensing; Soil moisture; Wireless sensor networks; A relay; Am2302 soil moisture sensor; An arduino uno; Daily lives; Esp8266; Farming system; Irrigation systems; Middle class; Soil moisture sensors; Water-saving; Internet of things
AB  - The goal of this research is to develop a smart irrigation system based on the Internet of Things that is affordable for a typical middle-class farmer. In the 21st century, automation has been more integrated into daily life. As a result of automation, machines may be programmed to operate without human intervention. The reduced energy use, increased productivity, and decreased amount of time spent on mundane tasks are all bonuses. In addition to being crucial to people's ability to eat and stay alive, agriculture is critical to a nation's economic development and overall prosperity. With the help of innovations like the Internet of Things (IoT), wireless sensor networks (WSNs), remote sensing, drone surveillance, and more, 'fastidious agriculture' has emerged as a hot topic in the IT industry. In this study, we demonstrate the efficacy of our gadget, which is designed to give data and save water by only watering fields when they really need it. Cloud website named item talk is used to transmit the data to the farmers. The farmers may keep tabs on their fields with the use of thing speaks channel connection. A built-in Wi-Fi module in the microcontroller transmits the collected data to the thing talk cloud storage service. Then, we'll evaluate how much water is saved in various soil types, such as sand, clay, and sandy clay. The water savings from this technology are clearly visible when compared to the water savings from the conventional technique. © 2023 IEEE.
