TI  - Enabling Digital Platforms: Toward Smart Agriculture
T2  - Artificial Intelligence Techniques in Smart Agriculture
LA  - English
KW  - Big Data analytics; Cloud computing; Disease surveillance; Feed and water management; Genomic sequencing; Health monitoring; Smart agriculture; Soil moisture; Weather forecasts
AB  - Smart, sustainable farming is a new way of doing things that uses cutting-edge technologies like the Internet of Things (IoT), cloud computing, and big data analytics to improve farming practices and care for the environment. Farmers can get real-time information about important things like soil moisture, temperature, and crop health by putting IoT monitors in their fields. This information is sent to cloud computing platforms, which have the computer power and storage needed to handle and analyze huge amounts of agricultural data. Algorithms for analyzing big data find patterns, trends, and connections. This lets farmers make smart choices about where to put resources, how to get rid of pests, how much water to use, and how to handle their livestock. Digital technologies like agricultural robots, drones, and mobile apps like farm calculators and cattle tracking can make farming more efficient, sustainable, and productive by giving farmers information on how to grow crops and reducing risks and uncertainties like diseases, pests, and drought. Real-time tracking and predictive analytics make it possible for farmers to find and fix potential problems quickly, which protects crops and increases production. By combining past and real-time data, farmers can learn important things about crop yields, market demand, and climate-related risks. This gives them the tools they need to plan their operations well and respond to changes in the market. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2024.
