KW  - Fifth Generation; Graphics Processing Unit; Key Performance Indicator; Quality of Service; Radio Access Network; 5G mobile communication systems; Information management; Network performance; Radio access networks; Resource allocation; Crop managements; Crop monitoring; Fifth generation; Graphics processing; Key performance indicators; Network slicing; Precision Agriculture; Processing units; Quality-of-service; Radio access networks; Efficiency
AB  - Modern technologies help precision agriculture by improving weather predictions, crop management, and crop monitoring. To improve these farming techniques, this research develops a system that integrates a 5G network connection. The system consists of Network Slicing for Agricultural Applications, a Weather Forecasting Module, and a system for managing and monitoring crops. Real-time data on crop health, nitrogen levels, soil moisture, and other factors are gathered by the Crop Monitoring System for insect infestations using drones and IoT sensors. ML algorithms are then used to evaluate the data. For localized weather predictions, the Weather Forecasting Module uses sophisticated time series models and distributed weather stations. Network slicing guarantees the best possible resource allocation and service quality for a range of agricultural applications. The suggested system achieved a prediction accuracy of 92.5%, resource utilization efficiency of 88.3%, data latency of 25 ms, average bandwidth of 50 Mbps, latency for real-time monitoring of 15 ms, and overall network performance of 89.8%, indicating significant improvements as shown by the experimental results. These results show that by integrating 5G, the suggested solution significantly increases the efficacy and efficiency of precision agriculture. © 2024 IEEE.
