AU  - Kanaga Priya, P.; Reethika, A.; Vijaykumar, G.; James Deva Koresh, H.
TI  - Robotics-assisted precision and sustainable irrigation, harvesting, and fertilizing processes
T2  - Hyperautomation in Precision Agriculture: Advancements and Opportunities for Sustainable Farming
LA  - English
KW  - fertilization; harvesting; precision; Robotics; sustainable
AB  - This book chapter dives into the use of robotics in farming practices. The main focus will be on how they can be used in precision agriculture, specifically when irrigation, harvesting, and fertilization come into play. Agriculture needs to make a change towards more precise and sustainable practices so that one can continue to grow crops without wasting resources by using robots. By introducing them to key processes like these it is expected to have better efficiency, resource usage, and sustainability overall. The chapter starts by taking a look into the importance of precision agriculture as a whole and how it addresses challenges faced by the industry. To ensure optimal crop growth this level of preciseness is needed otherwise the resource wastage will be at risk. To help with all of this, autonomous systems and drones have been introduced to these practices. Let’s not forget robotic arms too since they are also vital in achieving our objectives. Firstly though, the chapter takes a look at robotics-assisted precision irrigation and talks about how autonomous systems and sensor technologies deliver water exactly where and whenever it is needed. Robotic irrigation systems can monitor soil moisture levels, assess crop water requirements, and adjust irrigation schedules accordingly. By optimizing water usage, farmers can use it more efficiently during crop production. This will reduce wastage and be a sustainable way of growing crops. Next, the chapter talks about using robots to pick these crops. Robotic harvesters with computer vision, machine learning, and robotic arm technology can identify the ripest crops, select them with precision, and even sort and package them. Not only does this speed up harvesting while reducing labor needs, but it also ensures consistent quality and less damage to each crop. The chapter also looks into crop variability and adapting to different crop types for these robots. It then continues on robotics by looking into their use in fertilization processes. Robots can put just the right amount of fertilizer needed for each plant. They can do this by analyzing soil conditions with sensors and AI algorithms. While doing so they’re minimizing environmental pollution by preventing overuse of harmful chemicals as well as waste from those chemicals. These two things usually make up most of the environmental pollution associated with farming. The chapter also addresses the challenges that come with implementing robotics. While technology has undoubtedly improved our world, it isn’t perfect. There are many things to think about before starting to use robots in agriculture. Technological restrictions, expenses, and the need for training and maintenance are just a few issues that are faced. The chapter also takes note of the ethical and regulatory aspects of introducing robotics into agriculture. Looking at risks like safety and privacy, and how they can negatively affect rural communities. The chapter concludes on a positive note discussing the horizon for robotics-assisted precision and sustainable agriculture. There’s plenty of potential when it comes to AI, sensor technologies, and robotic advancements in helping irrigation, harvesting, and fertilization processes become more precise and sustainable. This chapter doesn’t just emphasize the potential benefits though; it explores everything from applications to challenges that’ll stand in our way of achieving an efficient ecosystem using these technologies. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
